course: dictionary
lesson: 9139
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one is right. Snatch is one of them. «He snatched the ball from me» means he grabbed it quickly and roughly. Read the words around it to decide.
explain: Snatch can also be a noun, meaning a small part of something you hear. «I heard a snatch of a song from the next room» means only a little bit of the song, not the whole thing. The word of tells you a part is coming.
explain: Snatch changes its ending to match the time: snatches, snatched, snatching. Use the swap check: put your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
fact: Snatch means to grab something quickly and roughly, or to take it quickly when you get the chance.
fact: «A thief snatched her bag» means the thief stole it by grabbing it.
fact: As a noun, a snatch is a small part of something you hear, like «a snatch of a song».
fact: Gulls on a seaside promenade may snatch chips right out of a child's hand.
fact: Useful phrases are snatch away, snatch up and bag snatcher. In crowds, hold your bag and phone close, because thieves may snatch them.
exercise: «He snatched the ball from me.» What does snatched mean here? | *Grabbed quickly and roughly | Dropped it on the grass | Gave it to me gently | Kicked it high into the air
exercise: «A thief snatched her bag.» What did the thief do? | Left the bag behind | Gave it back to her | *Stole it by grabbing it | Ran away from the park
exercise: Find the word that shows a gull took a chip quickly: «A gull swoops down and snatches a chip from the child's hand.» | hand | gull | chip | *snatches
test: «I heard a snatch of a song from the next room.» What is a snatch in this sentence? | *A small part of something you hear | A loud shout | A whole song played slowly | A dance
test: Ella says snatch always means steal. Is she right? | Yes, it always means steal | No, it means sleep | Yes, but only when someone is at the seaside | *No, it can also mean grab quickly, take a chance, or a small part of something
exam: What should you do if a little child grabs something sharp? | Shout loudly and run away | *Calmly swap it for a toy and get an adult | Snatch it by the blade so it is gone quickly | Let the child keep it
